{"questions":[{"question_id":"1l2rnupg5","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2011 Paper 1 Offline","year":2011,"examDate":2011,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"s-block-elements","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"compounds-of-alkali-metals","type":"integer","marks":3,"negMarks":0,"permalink":"preaction-of-brsub2sub-with-nasub2subcosub3s-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-dgpwskrl58u5cfcx","content":"<p>Reaction of Br<sub>2</sub> with Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> in aqueous solution gives sodium bromide and sodium bromate with evolution of CO<sub>2</sub> gas. The number of sodium bromide molecules involved in the balanced chemical equation is ________.</p>","options":[],"correct_options":[],"explanation":"<p>The balanced chemical equation is</p>\n<p>3Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 3Br<sub>2</sub> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> 5NaBr + NaBrO<sub>3</sub> + 3CO<sub>2</sub></p>"}]}